    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We document the length and angular relationships between faults and their splays in the Aztec Sandstone in the Valley of Fire State Park (SE Nevada) from detailed field measurements. Results show that the length of the longest splay produced by a fault is correlated to the fault length by a power law, but is not correlated with fault slip. The kink angle between faults and splays is influenced by the fault configuration. Isolated faults lead to small kink angles (average ~20°) whereas interacting faults result in systematically larger kink angles (average ~50°).\r\n\r\nWe use mechanical models based on the concept of cohesive end zone to interpret the variations in the kink angle distributions established in the field. Models of isolated faults show that small kink angles correspond to faults that make a small angle with the maximum compressive stress whereas large kink angles require a greater angle of the fault with the maximum stress. Models of interacting faults show that fault overlap and fault separation may induce strong variations in the kink angle that can explain the variations in our field data.\r\n\r\nThe knowledge acquired in terms of length and angular relationships between faults and splays may permit to estimate how far and in which direction splays propagate from their parent faults. This provides a critical insight into the prediction of secondary faults associated with faults with resolvable offset in the subsurface.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
